# UPDATE 5 (2026-08-22): 1b9432474daa is the first pin that fixes WRONG
# encodings rather than adding missing ones, and the distinction matters.
#
#   * stb_dri / lda_dri had SWAPPED sub-opcodes. The ROM decides: `f3 07 e4 e0
#     31` is `lda XBC,XBC+WA`, and 0x31 = 0x30|reg, so 0x30 is LDA and 0x40 the
#     byte store -- as stw_dri=0x50 / stl_dri=0x60 already implied. You had to
#     write `stb_dri` to get an LDA, so 4,693 sites in this tree named a STORE
#     where the CPU computes an ADDRESS. Bytes right, meaning wrong.
#   * st_rrw / st_rrl both carried 0x40 under a comment claiming OpSize picks
#     the sub-opcode range. TLCS900MCCodeEmitter.cpp only does that for
#     Opcode < 0xF0, and this prefix is 0xF3 -- so both silently emitted the
#     BYTE sub-opcode. Now spelled out per size.
#
# ⚠ MIGRATION REQUIRED, and it is not just a rename. stb_dri takes an 8-bit
# register class and lda_dri a 32-bit one, so those 4,693 sites were written
# with 8-BIT REGISTER NAMES for 32-bit LDA destinations. The mapping was
# measured, not assumed, by encoding all sixteen names:
#     W<->XWA(0) A<->XBC(1) B<->XDE(2) C<->XHL(3)
#     D<->XIX(4) E<->XIY(5) H<->XIZ(6) L<->XSP(7)
# so `stb_dri A` (0x31) becomes `lda_dri XBC` (0x31) -- same byte, right name.
# Migrated 5,674 sites across SEVEN trees: v7, v9, v10, table_data, hdae5000,
# v142 and subcpu. ⚠ The first attempt missed v142 and subcpu, and the failure
# surfaced as an LZSS "input does not match the reference compression
# decisions" error on a DIFFERENT ROM, with no assembler error anywhere. Only
# that step's refusal to fall back kept it from producing a wrong ROM quietly.
#
# Session tally, corrected: THREE genuinely missing forms added, FIVE claims of
# "the backend cannot do this" that were spellings not tried, and TWO silent
# misencodings. The misencodings are the dangerous class -- a missing form
# fails loudly at assembly time, a wrong sub-opcode assembles and lies.
#
# Verified with a full `make clean-all && make all` on this pin: 9/9 at 100.00%.

# UPDATE 6 (2026-08-23): bcf152d1fe00 adds cpib_ind, the register-indexed
# compare with an immediate. The STORE direction existed (STIB_DRI, 0xF3 prefix,
# sub-opcode 0x00); the COMPARE direction had no mnemonic, so `cp (XIX+IZ),0x0c`
# -- c3 07 f0 f8 3f 0c in v7 -- could not be written in any spelling. CP8mi
# covers `cp (r+d),imm` through MEMri and collapses to the bare core `3f 0c`.
#
# ⚠ OpSize is pinned to 8-bit. The emitter adjusts the prefix by size for any
# Opcode < 0xF0 (C3/D3/E3); STIB_DRI escapes that only because 0xF3 >= 0xF0, so
# copying its shape emitted 0xE3. Same mechanism as the st_rrw/st_rrl defect
# fixed in UPDATE 5 -- I had documented it that morning and still needed the
# encoding to catch me.
#
# Session tally, final: FOUR genuinely missing forms added (ldb/ldw/ldl_dri,
# LDS_ERPB, LD_ERPB_RR, cpib_ind), TWO silent misencodings fixed, and SEVEN
# claims of "the backend cannot do this" that were spellings not tried -- plus
# one that was a converter lookup table holding 32 of 256 register bytes. The
# suspicion is wrong far more often than it is right; one llvm-mc call settles it.
#
# Verified: all six rebuilt ROMs byte-identical via
# scripts/analysis/assert_byte_identical.py (NOT a rounded percentage).

# UPDATE 7 (2026-09-01): moved to tlcs900_backend@f29a4827693f, two commits on
# top of bcf152d1fe00 (e7a43c67fdca then f29a4827693f).  Together they close the
# gap that made wsa1/include/tlcs900_mem_ops.inc necessary at all.
#
#   * A DIRECT memory operand was always emitted as the 24-bit prefix, and only
#     for a 32-bit operand -- byte and word were refused outright.  All three
#     sizes encode now, and the ADDRESS WIDTH is requested in the source as
#     `(0x2075:16)`.  ⚠ The width is NOT derivable from the address value: this
#     firmware writes `set 7,(0x00008a)` as F2 8A 00 00 BF for an address that
#     fits in eight bits.  An operand with no suffix keeps the 24-bit default,
#     which is why nothing that already assembled changed.
#   * TWO SILENT MISCOMPILES.  `push (0x1234)` emitted `09 34` -- the address
#     truncated to eight bits with no diagnostic -- and `mul WA,(0x1234)`
#     emitted `d8 08 34 12`, a multiply by the ADDRESS.  Neither mnemonic had a
#     memory form, so the parenthesised address was read as an immediate.
#   * Twelve instructions that answered only to internal names (ormi8, addmi8,
#     incm8, ...) now take their real mnemonic; every old spelling is kept as an
#     InstAlias because this tree writes them in hundreds of files.
#
# ★ PROOF, not "llvm-mc accepted it".  wsa1/notes/llvm/llvm_native_equivalence.py
#   assembles BOTH spellings of every macro call site in the tree and compares
#   the bytes: 9,901 of the 12,539 sites are byte-identical under a real
#   mnemonic, 0 differ.  The remaining 2,638 are the register-indexed (rr+r)
#   operand, the register-direct operand and the two block transfers, for which
#   no native spelling is claimed.
#
# ⚠ AND ONE NEW DEFECT FOUND, not fixed: `ld wa,(xix+iz)` is ACCEPTED and emits
#   the (Xrr+d16) form with `iz` swallowed as an UNDEFINED SYMBOL -- d3 f1 00 00
#   20 where the ROM has d3 07 f0 f8 20.  It fails at link time only because
#   nothing defines `iz`.  See wsa1/notes/llvm/llvm_encoding_gaps.py, class C.
#
# Verified: `make gate-all` -- 6 KN5000 ROMs and 4 SX-WSA1R ROMs, all IDENTICAL,
# with the toolchain rebuilt from this pin.

# UPDATE 8 (2026-09-01): 95f7f2d40428 adds no encoding.  It turns the last two
# known SILENT MISCOMPILES into diagnostics, which is the outcome that matters
# when an encoding is not available:
#
#   * an immediate that fits its field neither signed nor unsigned was
#     truncated to the low bytes -- `push 0x1234` was [0x09,0x34], and
#     `cp (xhl),0x1234` was [0x83,0x3f,0x34].  Both readings stay legal,
#     because the same field spells -1 and 0xFF;
#   * `(Xrr+Rn)`, the register-indexed operand, was read as `(Xrr+d16)` with
#     the index register swallowed as an UNDEFINED SYMBOL: `ld wa,(xix+iz)`
#     assembled to d3 f1 00 00 20 where the ROM has d3 07 f0 f8 20.
#
# ★ That the gate is still green with these checks in place is itself the
#   finding: NOTHING in either tree was relying on a truncated immediate.
#
# Verified: `make gate-all` -- 10 ROMs, all IDENTICAL, and llvm-lit clean on
# all 73 TLCS900 MC and CodeGen tests.

# UPDATE 9 (2026-09-01): 4149d0474bf8 closes the KN5000 sound residue.  Seven
# encodings the v1.42 sub-CPU payload contains had no spelling -- CP (mem),#imm8
# (sub-opcode 0x3F, the one member of the 0x38..0x3F row with no def, in BOTH
# the (Xrr) and the (Xrr+d8) table), ADD r32,(mem) and (mem+d8) (the whole
# r,(mem) direction of the LONG table), CP r16,(mem+d8), the register-indexed
# operand with an immediate, and LDA with a PREVIOUS-BANK index register.
#
# ⚠ AND ONE INVERTED ENCODING.  The 8-bit INDEX register's file address was
# emitted with its halves swapped.  The register file is byte-addressed and
# little-endian, so a word register's LOW half is at offset 0 -- A=0xE0, W=0xE1,
# C=0xE4, B=0xE5 -- and the emitter had A=0xE1.  The byte gate stayed green
# because all FOUR call sites in this tree named the wrong register to get the
# right byte; two of them index by a value the preceding instruction loads into
# A.  Corroborated by MAME unidasm over all 32 codes 0xE0..0xFF.
#
# ★ AND THE FINDING THAT COST THE LEAST WORK: of the 59 "unspellable" forms in
#   notes/sound/FINDINGS-kn5000-sound-coverage-2026-09-01.md, 48 already had a
